Use the non-throwing ev::Loop::run() and poll() in the LoopDriver thread.

The throwing overloads read the thread-local error left by the last failed
operation after the loop returns. An error that was already handled (an SDO
abort during a slave boot, a failed fopen) then surfaces as a
std::system_error out of the thread function when the loop is stopped for
teardown, and the process ends in std::terminate with "run: No such file or
directory". A loop that fails while not stopped is still reported, through
diag().

Applies to lely-core v2.3.3. Not submitted to lely-core yet; drop it once an
upstream release carries an equivalent fix.

diff --git a/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp b/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp
--- a/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp
+++ b/src/coapp/loop_driver.cpp
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #if !LELY_NO_COAPP_MASTER && !LELY_NO_THREADS
 
 #include <lely/coapp/loop_driver.hpp>
+#include <lely/util/diag.h>
 
 #include <atomic>
 #ifdef __MINGW32__
@@ -151,10 +152,16 @@
   auto exec = self->GetExecutor();
 
   // Start the event loop. Signal the existence of a fake task to prevent the
-  // loop for stopping early.
+  // loop for stopping early. The non-throwing overloads are used because the
+  // throwing ones report the error left by the last failed operation even when
+  // the loop was stopped normally, and an exception escaping this thread
+  // function terminates the process.
+  ::std::error_code ec;
   exec.on_task_init();
-  loop.run();
+  loop.run(ec);
   exec.on_task_fini();
+  if (ec && !loop.stopped())
+    diag(DIAG_ERROR, ec.value(), "LoopDriver event loop failed");
 
   // Deregister the driver to prevent the master from queueing new events. This
   // also cancels any outstanding SDO requests.
@@ -162,7 +169,8 @@
 
   // Finish remaining tasks, but do not block.
   loop.restart();
-  loop.poll();
+  // The error is ignored: the driver is already deregistered.
+  loop.poll(ec);
 
   // Satisfy the promise to signal that the thread is about to terminate and it
   // a call to the destructor will not block.
